---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { languagePaths, type LanguageProps } from "../../../i18n/routes";

const gpxSample = String.raw`<metadata>
  <name>2026-11-08</name>
  <extensions>
    <lr:cameraClock measuredAt="2026-11-08T21:14:05Z" cameraMinusUtcSeconds="-28843"/>
    <lr:shot time="2026-11-08T21:14:05Z"/>
  </extensions>
</metadata>`;

export const getStaticPaths = languagePaths;
const { lang } = Astro.props as LanguageProps;
---
<Base
  title="Adding GPS to my full-frame camera — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="My Sony A7II has no GPS. My phone records the track, measures the camera's clock, and LightPhotos puts every photo on the map without touching the RAW files."
  active="blogs"
  lang={lang}
  path="/blogs/adding-gps-to-my-full-frame-camera.html"
  translated={false}
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-11-11">November 11, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Adding GPS to my full-frame camera</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          My Sony A7II is a great full-frame camera with no idea where it
          is. My phone always knows. The trick is getting the two to agree on
          what time it is.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          My phone records a GPS track while I shoot and measures how far off
          the camera&rsquo;s clock is. LightPhotos matches each photo to the
          track by its capture time and keeps the location beside the photo.
          The RAW files are never changed.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>The camera doesn&rsquo;t know where it is</h2>
      <p>
        The A7II came out in 2014. It has no GPS and no Bluetooth, so the
        phone location link that newer Sony bodies have isn&rsquo;t an
        option. Every photo has a time and an exposure, and no place.
      </p>
      <p>
        I don&rsquo;t want a GPS box on the hot shoe. I already carry a phone
        with a good GPS in it. The idea is old and simple: record a track on
        the phone, then match each photo to the track by time. Most photo
        apps can do this with a GPX file.
      </p>
      <p>
        It works badly in practice because of the clock.
      </p>

      <h2>The clock is the hard part</h2>
      <p>
        The camera writes its own wall time into each photo:
        <code>2026:11:08 13:14:05</code>, with no time zone. The GPS track is
        in UTC. To match them, you need to know the difference between the
        camera&rsquo;s clock and UTC, to the second.
      </p>
      <p>Three things make that hard:</p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>The camera&rsquo;s clock drifts.</strong> At walking pace,
          30 seconds of error puts a photo 40 meters down the road.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>I forget to change the time zone.</strong> I travel, and
          the camera stays on home time for a week.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Daylight saving time repeats an hour.</strong> On the
          night the clocks go back, 01:30 happens twice. A wall time alone
          can&rsquo;t say which one.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Most geotagging tools ask you for one fixed offset and hope it
        holds for the whole trip.
      </p>

      <h2>The phone measures the camera</h2>
      <p>
        I wrote LightRemote, an iPhone remote for the A7II, which uses
        Sony&rsquo;s Camera Remote API over Wi-Fi. It gives me live view,
        interval shooting and bulb on the phone. When the phone connects to
        the camera, it also starts recording a precise GPS track. When the
        camera disconnects, it goes back to a low-power track.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because the phone tells the camera when to take each picture, it
        knows the exact UTC time of the shot. The camera sends back a
        preview JPEG, and that preview has the camera&rsquo;s own wall time
        in its EXIF. The difference between the two is the camera&rsquo;s
        clock offset, measured instead of guessed. It is measured again on
        every shot that the phone triggers, so drift is corrected as you go.
        Long exposures are left out, because the camera stamps when the
        exposure started, and the phone only knows when it ended.
      </p>
      <p>
        The phone can&rsquo;t fix the camera&rsquo;s clock: the A7II
        doesn&rsquo;t let you set it over Wi-Fi. So LightRemote writes each
        measurement into the track instead. The track is one GPX file per
        day in iCloud Drive, and the measurements sit in its metadata:
      </p>
      <pre class="lp-code">{gpxSample}</pre>
      <p>
        <code>-28843</code> means the camera reads 8 hours and 43 seconds
        behind UTC: Pacific time, and 43 seconds slow. A Live Activity on the
        lock screen shows that the track is recording and how far the camera
        clock is from the phone&rsquo;s, so I see a wrong time zone before I shoot a whole
        day with it.
      </p>

      <h2>LightPhotos does the matching</h2>
      <p>
        Back at the computer, I open the folder from the SD card in
        LightPhotos and choose <strong>Actions &rsaquo; Import GPS
        Tracks&hellip;</strong> in the Grid. I can pick GPX files, folders of
        them, or LightRemote&rsquo;s iCloud folder. Days that iCloud
        hasn&rsquo;t downloaded yet are downloaded first.
      </p>
      <p>
        The dialog has one row per camera, found by its model and serial
        number. Each row has two settings:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Time zone</strong>, set the way the camera&rsquo;s menu sets
          it: a UTC offset and a daylight saving switch.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Paired with LightRemote.</strong> Only a paired camera uses
          the track&rsquo;s measurements, because the track doesn&rsquo;t say
          which camera it measured. A camera that was never paired
          uses its time zone setting.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        LightPhotos remembers these rows, so the next import is one click.
        Then, for each photo:
      </p>
      <ol class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          Turn the camera&rsquo;s wall time into UTC with the nearest
          measurement. A measurement holds until the next one, even across
          days, so a measurement on Monday still places a photo on Thursday.
        </li>
        <li>
          Find that moment on the track. Between two GPS points at most 10
          minutes apart, it interpolates. Otherwise it uses a point within 5
          minutes, or leaves the photo alone. These are exiftool&rsquo;s
          rules, and LightRemote&rsquo;s own helper script passes the same
          test cases.
        </li>
        <li>
          Save the location in the photo&rsquo;s sidecar, with the GPX file
          it came from and the offset it used.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The import reads the folder in batches, off the main thread, so a
        card with thousands of RAW files doesn&rsquo;t freeze the app.
      </p>

      <h2>Your RAW files are never changed</h2>
      <p>
        Geotagging tools usually write GPS into the original files. I
        don&rsquo;t want any tool writing into my RAW files, so LightPhotos
        keeps the location in the sidecar, like every other edit. The Info
        panel shows it with its source. <strong>Actions &rsaquo; Clear GPX
        Locations</strong> removes it. A photo that already has its own GPS,
        from a phone or a newer camera, keeps its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        Location is private, so exports leave it out by default. In the
        export form, turn on <strong>Location</strong> and the stored
        position is written into the exported file&rsquo;s EXIF GPS block.
        If the source file has its own position, that one wins.
      </p>

      <h2>Without LightRemote</h2>
      <p>
        You don&rsquo;t need my phone app, or a Sony. Any GPX file works:
        from a running watch, a hiking app or a GPS logger. Without
        measurements in the file, LightPhotos uses the camera&rsquo;s time
        zone setting from the dialog. Set the camera clock carefully before
        a trip, and that is usually close enough.
      </p>

      <h2>When you can use it</h2>
      <p>
        Import GPS Tracks is in the desktop app on macOS, Windows and Linux,
        in v0.1.40. The iCloud folder is macOS only,
        because that is where LightRemote&rsquo;s tracks sync. The browser
        version can show a stored location, but can&rsquo;t import tracks.
      </p>
      <p>
        For me, the best part is that I don&rsquo;t have to think about it.
        The phone records when the camera connects, the camera clock is
        measured while I shoot, and the map is filled in at home.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/three-ways-to-cull-a-shoot-with-mcp.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Three ways to let AI cull a shoot</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/comfyui-for-local-ai.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">ComfyUI for local AI</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
